  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nClear skies today as high pressure slides down the lee side of\nthe Rockies into the Central Plains. With this we're seeing\nbreezy northerly winds advecting in cooler temperatures. \nCooling trend expected through the next couple of days. \n\nLooking at the upper-level pattern, broad upper-level troughing\nover the western 2/3rds of the country is helping the cooler \nair spread south out of Canada. The shortwave that exited our\narea this morning is helping to push the front we saw yesterday\ninto the Ohio River Valley this afternoon. Transient weak \nridging is building into our area ahead of the next shortwave \ntrough forecast to arrive Tuesday. Temperatures today will only \nwarm into the low-to-mid 40s. As winds calm tonight we'll see \ntemperatures plummet, dropping into the upper teens to mid 20s \nby Monday morning.\n\nThe chilly start on Monday will help keep temperatures cooler,\nwith highs forecast in the mid 30s to low 40s for the afternoon.\nHigh clouds start to move in Monday afternoon ahead of the\nsystem on Tuesday. Cloudy skies expected Monday night into\nTuesday with winds shifting to south or southeasterly. Despite\nthe shift to southerly winds, cloud cover will make it difficult\nfor temperatures to warm on Tuesday with highs in the upper 30s\nto low 40s. \n\nMoisture advection will be very anemic ahead of the weak \nclipper Low forecast to move in on Wednesday. There may be just\nenough for some sprinkles or flurries over NE Nebraska into \nnorthwestern Iowa, so have added this potential to the forecast\nfor Wednesday. Some ensemble members bring these sprinkles/flurries\nas far south as Omaha/Council Bluffs, but leaving this out for \nnow as model soundings really don't support this chance. May see\nthis introduced in later forecast packages though if we see a\nbit more saturation of the column. Cloudy skies will keep \ntemperatures cool with morning lows in the upper teens to mid \n20s and afternoon highs in the upper 30s to low 40s. \n\nThursday we see a strong jet streak push into the Intermountain\nWest buckling up a ridge over the Central Plains. This will \nincrease warmer southwesterly low-level flow into our region \nlending to a warming trend Thursday and Friday. It starts on \nThursday with clearer skies and weak southwesterly surface winds\nallowing temperatures to warm back into the low-to-mid 40s for \nhighs. We'll see stronger southwesterly flow aloft move in on\nFriday ahead of our next trough moving in Friday night. This\nwill warm temperatures into the mid 40s to low 50s . \n\nEnsembles drastically increase in member spread (lower\nconfidence) going into next weekend as they try and resolve the\ntiming and track of the next trough. The NBM has the mean \nkeeping temperatures warmer going into the weekend, while an \nearlier arrival and more southerly track of the trough cools \ntemperatures back down over the Holiday Weekend. All show \nlittle-to-no chance for precipitation with this next system.  \nLow-level dry air continues to eat away at any chance for \nprecipitation to reach the ground.",
